WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Film Courage: What mistakes do&nbsp;
writers make when writing characters?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:06.720
Robert Rippberger, Filmmaker: I think the mistakes&nbsp;
that writers make when writing characters is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:19.120
everybody kind of sounds like themselves or just&nbsp;
sounds too flat or that they put too much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:25.920
characterization into the description and&nbsp;
that's ultimately just a tiny tiny sliver of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.040
that comes so much from the dialogue and how they&nbsp;
interact with people and what they say in response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:41.040
to a line if somebody says how is your day and&nbsp;
then they completely take it off topic or they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:48.000
they fire back at them or it's it's it's the way&nbsp;
in which these interactions happen and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.440
ultimately film is about representing vitality and&nbsp;
representing life and so characters too should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.320
as engaging as possible and as fired&nbsp;
up as possible and I think sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.480
sometimes it's maybe playing it a little bit&nbsp;
too safe with with certain characters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.200
and having the freedom to really really&nbsp;
go out there and really really be bold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.360
are there certain screenwriters who you love&nbsp;
their dialogue whether it's understated or or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:26.000
like an Aaron Sorkin where it's so there's such a&nbsp;
performance from each line it's great to listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.520
certainly Aaron Sorkin is that I mean when anybody&nbsp;
talks about dialogue it's hard to hard to not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:39.040
zero in on him and what's phenomenal about him is&nbsp;
how the exposition can roll on and on and on but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.840
you're still there right and he also is so great&nbsp;
at non-sequiturs and then sort of bringing it back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:52.320
home about why this particular story was important&nbsp;
or why this was said and the nuance to language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.920
that he brings with with different characters of&nbsp;
why they say certain things and how it's crafted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.640
is is really I think something to to be admired&nbsp;
and studied what questions should a writer be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.840
asking themselves about their characters while&nbsp;
they're writing it I think the one chief question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:16.560
writers should be asking themselves&nbsp;
constantly is is this true to life is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:26.560
somebody I know is this what is the is this&nbsp;
reality and then if if there is another question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.160
it's is this somebody I would want to spend&nbsp;
time with for an hour and a half in a theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:37.040
it's and it could also be there's so many&nbsp;
ways to answer that it could be like well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.040
they're they're kind of a dumpster fire&nbsp;
but that could be entertaining like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.040
but but then also weaving that it you always have&nbsp;
to for me you always have to look at it in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.360
in sort of the gestalt of how does it how&nbsp;
does it interact with these other parts so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.840
you obviously don't want to have somebody&nbsp;
who's a complete villain as you're trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:07.520
trying to get audiences to empathize or tell a&nbsp;
story about about somebody who's a hero right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:14.720
those things just won't match so it's also what's&nbsp;
the what's the intention with this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.560
and then is it somebody you would want to spend&nbsp;
time with and are they a real person that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:25.520
you could point to in the world right he's&nbsp;
a young adult she she was sort of a dumpster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:31.840
fire for a while in the movie and fascinating&nbsp;
journey to be on that and same Leaving Las Vegas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.400
so yeah there's there's some that are fascinating&nbsp;
to watch and yeah there's others that are painful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.280
to watch and it's like this is maybe a little&nbsp;
too much yeah I mean I think the reason we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:50.480
drawn to those types of characters is like often&nbsp;
the light is shining brightest before it goes out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.880
and and those are people really&nbsp;
on the brink who are struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:04.240
to struggling with their their own inner demons&nbsp;
and so their behavior is is just bombastic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:12.720
and yeah it's it's certainly certainly something&nbsp;
to watch how do you find out what a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:19.680
wants I think in looking what a character wants&nbsp;
it's a really tricky question and as a director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:28.800
I always will do that with the actors because we&nbsp;
need to be in alignment and ultimately I need them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.840
to be more connected to that than ultimately&nbsp;
me because that's what is a good performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.480
and and I think that comes with writing down&nbsp;
the facts writing down the facts of the script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:49.520
and sometimes the character might directly tell&nbsp;
you or the director or the the actor might might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.440
lie might say I want this and you can say well&nbsp;
look at these other facts right he may say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.800
he wants a good job but he's showing up every day&nbsp;
drunk right and so what is it that he does really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.440
want and and that is I think is finding the spine&nbsp;
and and and asking those questions and and kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:14.320
referencing them back to the facts in the script&nbsp;
and you said that dialogue you often let actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.880
bring their own like it doesn't have to be on the&nbsp;
nose in terms of what the script is and you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:27.600
that that that helps in terms of the believability&nbsp;
the chemistry yeah I'm always hiring actors who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.440
are bringing themselves doesn't mean they&nbsp;
can't play another character but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.400
that they're bringing their own good ideas and&nbsp;
we will workshop things and then we'll go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.840
and rewrite the script based off of what they've&nbsp;
